bibliograph-worker (0.2.21-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.21 release: the feed order index matches the feed's own order.

    - `0018-feed-order-nulls-last.sql` replaces
      `happyview_records_feed_order_idx` with the same key but
      `DESC NULLS LAST`. The edge pages `com.atproto.feed.getFeedSkeleton` with
      `DESC NULLS LAST` (a record with no `createdAt` sorts last), and a plain
      `DESC` keyset defaults to `NULLS FIRST`, so the index could never supply
      the order: every page read all three activity collections and sorted them
      — 8.5 s measured on the production catalogue, against 31 ms once the
      index matches. The file converges: it only drops an index whose
      definition lacks `NULLS LAST`, so later deploys skip it.
    - `0003-feed-read.sql` now creates the same `NULLS LAST` definition, so a
      fresh database gets the usable index on its post-boot re-apply.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:26:00 +0000

bibliograph-worker (0.2.20-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.20 release: the edition shape becomes a maintained table, and the
    search predicates get trigram indexes.

    - `0015-materialize-edition-shape.sql` replaces the two aggregate views
      (`edition_identifier_shape`, `edition_publishers_shape`) with
      `edition_shape` and `edition_publisher_list`, kept current by a per-row
      refresh trigger on `edition_identifier_info` and
      `edition_identifier_publishers`. Both views keep their names, columns
      and order, so no reader changes; the join every catalog read performs is
      now a primary-key lookup instead of a `GROUP BY` over the whole claim
      table. The backfill runs only while a table is empty, so a re-applied
      file is a no-op, and an edition whose last claim goes away loses its row
      exactly as the aggregate view produced none.
    - `0016-search-indexes.sql` enables `pg_trgm` and adds the four trigram
      indexes the search match needs: `editions.title`,
      `coalesce(edition_shape.description, '')` (the expression the query
      compares), `contributors.name` and `edition_identifiers.value`. They are
      built with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, outside a transaction block, so
      the catalog stays writable while they build.
    - `0017-entity-search-indexes.sql` adds `publishers.name` and the two
      partial `(id) WHERE identifiers <> '[]'` indexes the contributor and
      publisher searches branch through.
    - All three are additive: no column or table is dropped, and the newest
      migration is `0017-entity-search-indexes.sql`.

